WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:07.270
HIRSH: Thank you all for coming to today's
Council on Foreign Relations meeting.

00:00:07.270 --> 00:00:15.019
The subject is the Great Society, its meaning
and impact.

00:00:15.019 --> 00:00:20.150
I think, over the course of modern American
history, there have been two great government

00:00:20.150 --> 00:00:27.420
revolutions, if you will, the New Deal and
the Great Society, and a major counter-revolution

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:33.570
and we're still dealing with the implications
of—of all of them.

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:39.510
The Great Society, which was so named by Lyndon
Johnson, first, in a speech he gave at the

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:48.699
University of Michigan in 1964, embraced an
extraordinary number of government programs

00:00:48.699 --> 00:00:57.039
that LBJ sought to use to address basically
what he thought were the deficiencies left

00:00:57.039 --> 00:01:04.970
over from the Great—from, rather, the New
Deal: Medicaid, Medicare, Head Start programs,

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:09.770
consumer and environmental protections, like
the—the Clean Air Act, the Voting Rights

00:01:09.770 --> 00:01:12.840
Act to deal with racial injustice.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:21.330
It was an extraordinary array of programs
enacted in—in a period of time that was

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:30.420
just as extraordinarily short, by the 89th
Congress, from January '65 to January of '67;

00:01:30.420 --> 00:01:34.100
basically that was when most of it was enacted.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:40.650
And that Congress has been deemed by historians
to have been probably the most productive

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.860
in American history.

00:01:42.860 --> 00:01:48.250
Grover certainly would—would disagree with
perhaps the term used there.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:56.420
And then for—you know, for those who doubt
the so-called Great Man theory of history,

00:01:56.420 --> 00:02:03.750
I think it's interesting to note that—that
LBJ's idol was FDR, was Franklin Roosevelt.

00:02:03.750 --> 00:02:11.810
He went into his accidental presidency, consciously
intending to live up to the ideals, as he

00:02:11.810 --> 00:02:16.709
saw it, that FDR had put in place in the New
Deal.

00:02:16.709 --> 00:02:23.230
And for a very brief period of time in American
history, LBJ, arguably, had as much power

00:02:23.230 --> 00:02:29.830
as FDR did in the very beginning stages of
the New Deal, in terms of the—the vast number

00:02:29.830 --> 00:02:38.689
of Democrats that came to Congress and the
impact of the 1964 election, in which an uber-conservative

00:02:38.689 --> 00:02:43.840
named Barry Goldwater was—was badly beaten
in the worst landslide in American History,

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.230
sending the conservatives for cover for the
same period when many of these programs were

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:49.230
enacted.

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:58.200
But, I mean, just as so many of these programs
continue today, are debated today and, in

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:05.480
fact, I—you know, we're going to lace our
discussion today with the State of the Union

00:03:05.480 --> 00:03:11.719
address by the President last night, in which
Obama, you know, kind of, in some respects,

00:03:11.719 --> 00:03:18.409
laid out his own vision for addressing deficiencies
left over, as he perceives it, from what hasn't

00:03:18.409 --> 00:03:20.920
worked since the Great Society.

00:03:20.920 --> 00:03:27.230
But as the—the concept of the Great Society
lives on, so does the backlash, the counter-revolution,

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:28.939
if you will.

00:03:28.939 --> 00:03:34.489
We all are somewhat familiar with how it ended,
overspending, Vietnam, the—the disaster

00:03:34.489 --> 00:03:41.620
of the late Johnson Presidency, followed by—into
the 1970s, the period of stagflation, all

00:03:41.620 --> 00:03:47.950
of which, of course, laid the groundwork for
the Reagan revolution, which is the counter-revolution

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:49.909
I was referring to.

00:03:49.909 --> 00:03:55.161
And that way of thinking, right up to the
present day, right up to the Tea Party, is

00:03:55.161 --> 00:04:03.439
also a legacy that we're dealing with, even
though Reagan himself failed to roll back

00:04:03.439 --> 00:04:04.799
government.

00:04:04.799 --> 00:04:07.299
No other Republican president has.

00:04:07.299 --> 00:04:13.230
We know Richard Nixon, for example, after
taking over from LBJ, quickly sought the center

00:04:13.230 --> 00:04:15.459
and embraced many Great Society programs.

00:04:15.459 --> 00:04:22.120
And of course, right up to President George
W. Bush, who not only did not roll back Medicare,

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.770
he added prescription drugs to that program.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:31.460
So in so many respects, despite the rhetoric
of counter-revolution, we have seen a failure

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:34.570
to really roll back the Great Society and
that's one of the things we're going to be

00:04:34.570 --> 00:04:36.170
discussing today.

00:04:36.170 --> 00:04:43.030
And for that discussion, we could not have
two—a better cast of characters—let me

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:49.442
put it that way, than Governor Howard Dean,
former governor of Vermont, of course, and

00:04:49.442 --> 00:04:55.800
former chairman of the Democratic National
Committee, who will be taking the affirmative--no.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:01.600
And Grover Norquist is president for the Americans
for Tax Reform, but is known to all of us,

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.980
perhaps, and has been for many years as what
I would say is the chief scold of the—of

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:14.501
the Republicans and conservatives in Washington,
in terms of—of his attempts to—to continue

00:05:14.501 --> 00:05:16.630
to roll back government.

00:05:16.630 --> 00:05:23.260
And I—even though what we have here may
be more in the nature of a debate than a typical

00:05:23.260 --> 00:05:27.880
CFR discussion, we are going to try to keep
it a discussion as much as possible, rather

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:31.880
than turning it into something that might
foreshadow what we're going to see in the

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:34.030
2016 Presidential campaign.

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:37.920
So, with that, I want to start out.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:43.600
Governor Dean, perhaps you could just give
us your overall assessment of the impact of

00:05:43.600 --> 00:05:47.220
the Great Society then and now and—and how
its—its legacy lingers on.

00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.220
DEAN: Sure.

00:05:48.220 --> 00:05:50.940
This is going to be a hard one to—so you're
going to have to cut me off and I'm not going

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:52.200
to be offended if you do.

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:53.490
HIRSH: I'll (inaudible).

00:05:53.490 --> 00:05:59.950
DEAN: So, let me—I actually, now, as part
of my many part-time jobs, teach foreign policy

00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:03.480
at Yale at the Jackson Institute, which means
I also have to teach American history, because

00:06:03.480 --> 00:06:06.710
a lot of kids don't know a lot about American
history.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:11.020
And I've learned a lot, just preparing for
the—for the class.

00:06:11.020 --> 00:06:15.600
I—I hated Lyndon Johnson.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.810
I was a student in college when he was lying
to us about the Vietnam War.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:24.190
And I was a fairly low number in the draft.

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:28.420
In fact, I actually had the physical and flunked
it.

00:06:28.420 --> 00:06:32.750
And I now believe Lyndon Johnson was one of
the five great presidents in American history,

00:06:32.750 --> 00:06:35.980
simply because I judge presidents by what
they achieved and the—and the changes they

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:42.080
made and the changes have been extraordinary.

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:46.660
I also look at this from a sort of a human
evolutionary point of view.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:51.850
I think, while we all have a libertarian streak,
particularly in America, whether we're Democrats

00:06:51.850 --> 00:06:52.850
or Republicans.

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:54.130
We also have a communitarian streak.

00:06:54.130 --> 00:06:57.860
And over the last 10,000 years of American
history, I think we've become more - I mean

00:06:57.860 --> 00:07:01.010
of—of human history, I think we've become
more communitarian.

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:09.370
So I actually see—see the—the—the Roosevelt,
the New Deal and the Great Society as an evolutionary

00:07:09.370 --> 00:07:13.990
movement in the world, because of course it's
happened in other countries as well, which

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:19.980
means we—we actually—it's a—it's a fundamental
difference in philosophy that I think we have

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:23.510
with—with the Republicans and the true libertarians.

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:28.320
I believe that we are recognizing, as human
beings, that we have a responsibility to each

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:29.480
other.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:33.970
It's not just every person for themselves,
that bald capitalism without rules doesn't

00:07:33.970 --> 00:07:38.510
work and that there have to be a set of rules
to make capitalism work.

00:07:38.510 --> 00:07:43.930
And the question is then, in the constraints
of what we've agreed to, how do you make it

00:07:43.930 --> 00:07:45.030
work.

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:47.100
So I think there never has been a counter-revolution.

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:52.510
There's a lot of talk about the Reagan revolution,
but as Michael pointed out, there's not been

00:07:52.510 --> 00:07:54.350
much real change.

00:07:54.350 --> 00:07:57.100
And I think—I happen to be fiscally conservative.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:01.160
Grover would probably disagree with that,
but actually I was—I was once ranked fourth

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:07.720
in the country in terms of fiscal responsibility
when I was governor and they told me they

00:08:07.720 --> 00:08:11.190
had to redo the results five times before
they'd release them and I was still number

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:13.240
four in the country in terms of my conservatism.

00:08:13.240 --> 00:08:19.530
They couldn't believe it, but I think fiscal
conservativism is actually—is actually compatible

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.930
with a—an aspect of responsibility for each
other, 'cause you can't—if you can't fund

00:08:23.930 --> 00:08:26.980
the programs, you shouldn't have them and
you eventually have to cut them.

00:08:26.980 --> 00:08:29.760
So I do think Johnson transformed society.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:34.630
I think it was part of an evolutionary transformation
and I think it's for the better.

00:08:34.630 --> 00:08:36.380
Now, do we have to fix some of these programs?

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:38.000
Are they going to make us broke?

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.000
Yes.

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.159
We do have to fix some of these programs.

00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:42.659
I am very interested in how we fix them.

00:08:42.659 --> 00:08:44.330
I'm not interested in getting rid of them.

00:08:44.330 --> 00:08:46.249
I'm not interested in privatizing them.

00:08:46.249 --> 00:08:48.740
I'm not interested in turning them back to
the states.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:53.529
And I think there'll be a lot of discussion
today about what we should do to fix these

00:08:53.529 --> 00:08:54.529
programs.

00:08:54.529 --> 00:08:58.370
And I will just say, leading into what—whatever
Grover may say in rebuttal, something that

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:03.580
he's probably going to agree with, we—we
cannot continue on the current fiscal course

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:04.950
that we're on.

00:09:04.950 --> 00:09:09.620
My view is that we ought to, as Bill Clinton
famously said about welfare, we ought to mend

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:12.029
it and not end it.

00:09:12.029 --> 00:09:13.190
HIRSH: Grover?

00:09:13.190 --> 00:09:14.360
NORQUIST: Yeah.

00:09:14.360 --> 00:09:18.800
You mentioned the two periods of—of—of
government expansion.

00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:26.730
It's actually rather tight, the—between
the '34 and '36 election, you saw Social Security

00:09:26.730 --> 00:09:29.690
and Aid to Families with Dependent Children.

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:36.000
And between the '64 and '66 election, we saw
the Great Society spending programs, Medicaid

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:37.300
and Medicare.

00:09:37.300 --> 00:09:43.749
The federal government right now spends 20
percent of Americans earned this last year.

00:09:43.749 --> 00:09:50.940
It was 24 percent before we got the sequester
and wrestled the President on that but not

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:56.470
allowing him to raise taxes up to 24, but
to bring spending down to 20.

00:09:56.470 --> 00:10:06.069
But of the 20, half—10 percent of everything
you earn and half of federal spending was

00:10:06.069 --> 00:10:10.449
created in two two-year periods, so in four
years of American history.

00:10:10.449 --> 00:10:14.829
So when some of our friends on the left talk
about the consensus, if you grab power for

00:10:14.829 --> 00:10:21.550
two years at a time and run things through,
you can pass some very consequential legislation

00:10:21.550 --> 00:10:22.999
and they did.

00:10:22.999 --> 00:10:28.350
In '66, the Republicans won back in a wave
and then in '68 they did.

00:10:28.350 --> 00:10:31.730
This—we're supposed to be—we're supposed
to be in the middle of the third wave.

00:10:31.730 --> 00:10:37.649
This was Obama's hope that this would be another
period where we jump up government spending

00:10:37.649 --> 00:10:39.550
on a permanent basis.

00:10:39.550 --> 00:10:44.759
And it's very interesting to watch the different
reaction, both by Republicans and by the general

00:10:44.759 --> 00:10:50.649
electorate, after the creation of Obamacare
ACA.

00:10:50.649 --> 00:10:55.300
The public remains opposed to it and remains
unhappy with it, would like to see it dramatically

00:10:55.300 --> 00:11:00.720
changed, altered, gotten rid of, and it didn't
get a single Republican vote.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:06.309
Unlike the previous periods where you had
a bipartisan march towards bigger government,

00:11:06.309 --> 00:11:11.820
we now have in the Republican Party, post-Reagan
and post-Tea Party, a party committed to,

00:11:11.820 --> 00:11:12.820
actually, smaller government.

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:17.769
We didn't have that before, if you watched
eight years of Bush and eight—and four-and-a-half

00:11:17.769 --> 00:11:21.970
years of Republican Congresses during the
eight years of Bush's presidency.

00:11:21.970 --> 00:11:27.709
During that period, he just kept spending
money, and not spending money and spending

00:11:27.709 --> 00:11:29.779
less money wasn't even on the to-do list.

00:11:29.779 --> 00:11:35.260
When you talked to the guys there at the 'strategery
group', not spending more money wasn't ever

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:36.260
on the list.

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:39.350
They called—no, they called it the 'strategery
group' themselves, they at least had a sense

00:11:39.350 --> 00:11:43.740
of humor, not other things.

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:56.860
On the costs of the Great Society, well, we
spent $20 trillion on—since 1964, in inflation-adjusted

00:11:56.860 --> 00:12:03.860
dollars on welfare programs, 17 percent of
personal income in the United States now comes

00:12:03.860 --> 00:12:09.829
from means-tested welfare programs, not Social
Security, where you pay in and theoretically

00:12:09.829 --> 00:12:15.040
getting something back or Medicare, but where
you just get something because you have low

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:17.480
income with nothing else necessary.

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:26.490
This last year—'12—2012, $700 billion
spent but of course the real challenge is

00:12:26.490 --> 00:12:32.259
that we can't afford this very well now and
we certainly can't afford it in the future.

00:12:32.259 --> 00:12:35.649
Social Security has a $10.6 trillion unfunded
liability.

00:12:35.649 --> 00:12:38.759
That doesn't mean $10.6 trillion owed sometime.

00:12:38.759 --> 00:12:44.339
That's the present value of the unfunded liabilities
that we have in Social Security alone.

00:12:44.339 --> 00:12:50.759
Medicare, somewhere between $28 and $35 trillion
in unfunded liabilities.

00:12:50.759 --> 00:12:57.319
When Medicare was built, the costs in 1990
were 644 percent higher than they said they

00:12:57.319 --> 00:12:59.620
were going to be when the government put them
in.

00:12:59.620 --> 00:13:03.970
Turns out it's only 150 percent if you adjust
for inflation, so they're only off by 150

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:06.560
percent then.

00:13:06.560 --> 00:13:10.610
Looking forward, here's a quote from Paul
Ryan in 2011, talking about the Congressional

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:12.740
Budget Office, run by Democrats.

00:13:12.740 --> 00:13:16.889
"The CBO has a model where they measure the
economy going forward and they're telling

00:13:16.889 --> 00:13:20.560
us that the entire economy crashes in the
year 2037.

00:13:20.560 --> 00:13:26.329
Their computer simulator cannot conceive any
way in which the economy continues, given

00:13:26.329 --> 00:13:28.199
the entitlements that we have."

00:13:28.199 --> 00:13:29.199
2037.

00:13:29.199 --> 00:13:30.199
You can see that.

00:13:30.199 --> 00:13:32.920
Some of us may live to see it.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:39.369
And so when we talk about, you know, was this
a good idea, one, these are the costs, and

00:13:39.369 --> 00:13:44.720
then we get—you can have a longer, different
conversation about what the costs were in

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:51.270
human lives and families damaged and neighborhoods
damaged, and what benefits there were or weren't.

00:13:51.270 --> 00:13:55.050
And then the other thing to talk about, you
know, is, what else could we have done?

00:13:55.050 --> 00:13:59.040
Theoretically, everyone agrees we need to—we
need to change things before we hit the wall

00:13:59.040 --> 00:14:01.769
in 2037 or sooner.

00:14:01.769 --> 00:14:05.040
Why didn't we do this in 1965?

00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:07.749
Why are we waiting till now to do this?

00:14:07.749 --> 00:14:11.410
Why six years of Obama saying he wanted to
fix entitlements and nothing?

00:14:11.410 --> 00:14:14.339
He just added to entitlements, like Bush.

00:14:14.339 --> 00:14:21.749
So that's—as—as you look forward, you
can go through the—the—the costs and the

00:14:21.749 --> 00:14:27.499
alternatives as we move forward, because I—I
think we can fix things and I think we will.

00:14:27.499 --> 00:14:32.149
HIRSH: I mean, very briefly, before I give
Governor Dean a chance to respond to that,

00:14:32.149 --> 00:14:35.779
do you want to just sort of sum up what you
think were the opportunity costs?

00:14:35.779 --> 00:14:40.699
And again, we don't talk just purely in economic
numbers here, because this was a program to

00:14:40.699 --> 00:14:50.449
transform society specifically to deal with
income and racial divides, education divides,

00:14:50.449 --> 00:14:55.259
and a—you know, listening to the President
last night, he—his community college program.

00:14:55.259 --> 00:14:57.899
I mean, in a much smaller scale, he's trying
to deal with the same thing.

00:14:57.899 --> 00:15:02.749
So could you just briefly address what you
think our country would be like if we hadn't

00:15:02.749 --> 00:15:04.220
done the Great Society?

00:15:04.220 --> 00:15:13.610
NORQUIST: Well, I'm looking at the seven-minute
speech that LBJ gave when he announced at—at

00:15:13.610 --> 00:15:21.980
Ann Harbor the—the Great Society, this is
the same speech that Obama gave last night.

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:27.670
You could take the whole thing without changing
a word, because there are no dates, except

00:15:27.670 --> 00:15:28.670
fifty years from now.

00:15:28.670 --> 00:15:32.999
He has to be judged on it, by the way, in
fifty years.

00:15:32.999 --> 00:15:36.470
And they haven't exactly accomplished what
they wanted to.

00:15:36.470 --> 00:15:37.879
It's actually much more aggressive than you
said.

00:15:37.879 --> 00:15:40.850
I mean, they've—they've got plans for our
leisure time.

00:15:40.850 --> 00:15:45.290
They've got it—they're worried about unbridled
growth in the economy, have to do something

00:15:45.290 --> 00:15:46.290
about that.

00:15:46.290 --> 00:15:49.200
Love to have growth of any kind, bridled or
not.

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:54.019
I mean this is—we're going to be condemned
to a soulless wealth.

00:15:54.019 --> 00:15:55.489
Wealth to start with would be good.

00:15:55.489 --> 00:15:58.309
I mean this is just—I mean this is Savonarola.

00:15:58.309 --> 00:16:01.670
This is not a guy who just wants to increase
GDP two percent.

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:05.819
This is a guy who's in—in—going to be
in charge of your soul through the government,

00:16:05.819 --> 00:16:09.959
but we're going to do it by having working
groups and White House conferences and meetings,

00:16:09.959 --> 00:16:13.370
but the solutions to these problems do not
rest on a massive program in Washington.

00:16:13.370 --> 00:16:17.809
Really, what part of the thing that they did
wasn't a massive program based out of Washington.

00:16:17.809 --> 00:16:20.759
On, you know, damaging?

00:16:20.759 --> 00:16:27.480
Well, I passed out a comparison of what happened
when Reagan was president, in terms of workforce

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:28.480
participation.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:32.999
As the economy grew, more people came back
into the workforce and workforce participation

00:16:32.999 --> 00:16:34.029
grew.

00:16:34.029 --> 00:16:39.730
As this economy has recovered, workforce participation
has fallen, which is why the only reason the

00:16:39.730 --> 00:16:43.540
unemployment numbers are going down is because
of the number of people quitting looking for

00:16:43.540 --> 00:16:44.540
work.

00:16:44.540 --> 00:16:49.879
If you counted them in, it would be 10 or
11 percent as we move forward.

00:16:49.879 --> 00:16:57.290
The cost to the United States we have today—I—I
did pass out this chart.

00:16:57.290 --> 00:17:01.509
Poverty was falling up until they passed the
Great Society.

00:17:01.509 --> 00:17:05.630
And then, according to the government's own
statistics, static for fifty years or little

00:17:05.630 --> 00:17:08.620
higher than it was when they did it.

00:17:08.620 --> 00:17:11.809
The Governor wants to argue their statistics
or their definition of poverty (inaudible)

00:17:11.809 --> 00:17:12.809
they can.

00:17:12.809 --> 00:17:14.490
Those are the government's numbers.

00:17:14.490 --> 00:17:16.480
It is not something that was a problem.

00:17:16.480 --> 00:17:22.299
It wasn't getting better, it was getting significantly
better rapidly and then it stopped getting

00:17:22.299 --> 00:17:25.730
better after they did that.

00:17:25.730 --> 00:17:33.280
The number of men who left the workforce as
a result of this program and immediately after,

00:17:33.280 --> 00:17:38.980
16 percent of men over twenty were not working
or looking for work in 1965, when they started

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:41.130
passing these bills, it's 28 percent today.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:46.370
If you look at people between twenty—men
between twenty and sixty-four, six percent

00:17:46.370 --> 00:17:50.019
of the civilian, noninstitutionalized men
were out of the workforce when they passed

00:17:50.019 --> 00:17:51.019
these laws.

00:17:51.019 --> 00:17:53.559
Today it's 17 percent.

00:17:53.559 --> 00:17:58.200
The damage done to heads of households, the
damage done in how—what the government has

00:17:58.200 --> 00:18:04.830
done there, the lack of progress on the poverty
rate all suggest to me that it—and then

00:18:04.830 --> 00:18:11.669
you can track crime and government spending,
crime and the enaction of all these laws and

00:18:11.669 --> 00:18:12.669
make your own decisions.

00:18:12.669 --> 00:18:13.669
HIRSH: OK.

00:18:13.669 --> 00:18:18.470
Well, Governor, a lot of bold claims there,
starting with President Obama as Savonarola.

00:18:18.470 --> 00:18:19.549
Maybe you want to...

00:18:19.549 --> 00:18:22.659
NORQUIST: Oh, no, no, no, that was LBJ as
Savonarola.

00:18:22.659 --> 00:18:24.460
HIRSH: Ah, LBJ...

00:18:24.460 --> 00:18:25.460
(CROSSTALK)

00:18:25.460 --> 00:18:26.850
HIRSH: Obama's mini-Savonarola.

00:18:26.850 --> 00:18:28.649
Go ahead, sir.

00:18:28.649 --> 00:18:33.320
DEAN: You know, I—we're speaking a foreign
language here.

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:37.190
What I look at was why Social Security started
and why Medicare started.

00:18:37.190 --> 00:18:41.260
When Social Security was started, it was actually
started in the states, because the Depression,

00:18:41.260 --> 00:18:46.720
not—as we are not taught in school, did
not start with the 1929 stock market crash.

00:18:46.720 --> 00:18:51.291
It started in 1926, when the agriculture markets
began to crash all over the world, not just

00:18:51.291 --> 00:18:52.779
in the United States.

00:18:52.779 --> 00:18:58.350
And old people, who were surviving on their
farms, because—and they couldn't work anymore,

00:18:58.350 --> 00:19:01.500
had nothing and were literally in danger of
starvation.

00:19:01.500 --> 00:19:06.179
That's what—Kansas and Nebraska and places
like that is where Social Security started.

00:19:06.179 --> 00:19:10.820
What—what Roosevelt did with his 'let's
try—keep trying stuff until something works',

00:19:10.820 --> 00:19:15.110
which is basically what he said, is he looked
at other programs and these programs came

00:19:15.110 --> 00:19:20.419
from the states and he adopted them and created
a national program for Social Security.

00:19:20.419 --> 00:19:27.620
I think Social Security has been incredibly
important, because I think a—a—a minimum

00:19:27.620 --> 00:19:32.179
standard of living is important in this country.

00:19:32.179 --> 00:19:37.470
We have, in my view, the greatest problem
in this country today, that the greatest threat

00:19:37.470 --> 00:19:41.710
to democracy is the gap between those at the
top and those at the bottom.

00:19:41.710 --> 00:19:45.019
Not because I'm against making money, I'm
actually very interested in it.

00:19:45.019 --> 00:19:48.320
I think capitalism is the greatest system
we've ever—has ever been invented.

00:19:48.320 --> 00:19:51.870
But like every other system, if you have no
rules, the whole system collapses and it very

00:19:51.870 --> 00:19:54.330
nearly did in 2008.

00:19:54.330 --> 00:19:57.350
So if we're going to have a system where it's
possible to make large amounts of money, which

00:19:57.350 --> 00:20:01.740
it is, which I think is a good thing, we've
got to have a system where everybody believes

00:20:01.740 --> 00:20:02.740
that it works for them.

00:20:02.740 --> 00:20:04.809
And that's the danger to this country right
now.

00:20:04.809 --> 00:20:08.490
Eighty percent of the people in this country
have not seen a raise for the last twenty

00:20:08.490 --> 00:20:09.920
years.

00:20:09.920 --> 00:20:12.679
Almost all the wealth that's been accumulated
in this country, particularly coming out of

00:20:12.679 --> 00:20:16.269
this recession, has gone to the top 20 percent.

00:20:16.269 --> 00:20:20.100
Now the - I don't have a problem with people
being rich and people not being rich.

00:20:20.100 --> 00:20:23.571
I do have a problem with people not affording
the basic necessities, which we've more or

00:20:23.571 --> 00:20:25.289
less fixed in this country for—basically.

00:20:25.289 --> 00:20:28.740
We'd have arguments with some of my more liberal
friends about that.

00:20:28.740 --> 00:20:32.399
What I have a problem with is if 80 percent
of the people, the system—believe the system

00:20:32.399 --> 00:20:33.510
doesn't work for them.

00:20:33.510 --> 00:20:37.640
It doesn't matter if you argue that it does
work for them, because if they believe it

00:20:37.640 --> 00:20:39.750
doesn't, the whole system is dead.

00:20:39.750 --> 00:20:44.100
That would be a terrible shame for this extraordinary
country that we've built over the last 235

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:45.100
years.

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:47.419
So I think a—some sort of minimal standard
of living.

00:20:47.419 --> 00:20:51.631
I was the first governor in the country, by
two weeks, beating Tommy Thompson by two weeks,

00:20:51.631 --> 00:20:54.549
which I'm thrilled to say, who did welfare
reform.

00:20:54.549 --> 00:20:55.860
Why did we do welfare reform?

00:20:55.860 --> 00:20:58.590
Because I fundamentally believe you shouldn't
get something for nothing, unless you really

00:20:58.590 --> 00:20:59.900
are disabled.

00:20:59.900 --> 00:21:04.220
That's not the same as saying there shouldn't
be some kind of minimal standard where everybody

00:21:04.220 --> 00:21:05.399
can participate.

00:21:05.399 --> 00:21:08.809
The reason that healthcare costs have been
so outrageous and the Medicare projections,

00:21:08.809 --> 00:21:13.400
as Grover pointed out, are so outrageous has
very little to do with Medicare.

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:16.590
It has to do with the fact that the healthcare
industry is completely out of control.

00:21:16.590 --> 00:21:17.590
Why?

00:21:17.590 --> 00:21:19.540
Because the economics doesn't work and why
is that?

00:21:19.540 --> 00:21:23.070
Because you pay me to do as much stuff as
I possibly can to you, whether it works or

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:24.070
not.

00:21:24.070 --> 00:21:26.010
There's no other system in the world that
works like that.

00:21:26.010 --> 00:21:29.630
You've got to have a basic fee-per-patient
system.

00:21:29.630 --> 00:21:31.990
If you keep paying me to do stuff, I'm going
to keep doing it to you.

00:21:31.990 --> 00:21:35.090
And that's why the medical costs have gone
up at three times the rate of inflation.

00:21:35.090 --> 00:21:39.559
The reason Medicare is out of control is not
because Medicare is a bad program, it's because

00:21:39.559 --> 00:21:42.919
healthcare costs are out of control and we
have to be serious about dealing with healthcare

00:21:42.919 --> 00:21:43.919
costs.

00:21:43.919 --> 00:21:49.669
So I—I defend this system of basically a
minimal standard of living.

00:21:49.669 --> 00:21:54.600
Healthcare came in under Johnson because healthcare
costs were driving older people not to be

00:21:54.600 --> 00:21:56.780
able to be adequately taken care of.

00:21:56.780 --> 00:21:58.380
Life expectancy's gone up dramatically.

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:02.340
I won't claim it's only because of Medicare,
but that certainly had a lot to do with it.

00:22:02.340 --> 00:22:06.049
Medicaid has turned out to be the most effective
way of expanding access to healthcare.

00:22:06.049 --> 00:22:09.950
We used it twenty years ago in my state and
every kid in my state under eighteen has had

00:22:09.950 --> 00:22:12.200
health insurance for twenty years.

00:22:12.200 --> 00:22:16.700
So I am a—I am not a person who believes
we ought to keep spending more money on social

00:22:16.700 --> 00:22:23.610
programs and I agree in principle with the
dilemma that Grover laid out.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:25.019
We've got to deal with this stuff.

00:22:25.019 --> 00:22:29.120
We have a—a budget problem that isn't going
to go away unless we make some fundamental

00:22:29.120 --> 00:22:30.120
changes.

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:34.529
But I'm not philosophically interested in
dismantling the Great Society.

00:22:34.529 --> 00:22:39.260
HIRSH: Just before I let Grover respond to
that, I just wanted to follow up.

00:22:39.260 --> 00:22:43.799
I mean what you laid out, all the problems
we're dealing with today, starting with inequality,

00:22:43.799 --> 00:22:47.700
poverty, I mean doesn't that mean that the
Great Society failed in the end if it just...

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:52.850
DEAN: No, it means that—it means that the
rules of capitalism have been skewed.

00:22:52.850 --> 00:22:58.760
If you look at what von Hayek actually wrote,
the penalty in a capitalist society for making

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:00.919
big mistakes has to be failure.

00:23:00.919 --> 00:23:02.340
We don't have that penalty in this country.

00:23:02.340 --> 00:23:04.049
This is what Elizabeth Warren's talking about.

00:23:04.049 --> 00:23:07.669
I think that conservatives ought to love Elizabeth
Warren.

00:23:07.669 --> 00:23:12.779
It's perfectly plain to me, not because I'm
an Elizabeth Warren liberal, or you can call

00:23:12.779 --> 00:23:15.059
me whatever you want.

00:23:15.059 --> 00:23:18.620
It's perfectly plain to me that if you have
ten or fifeen or twenty institutions around

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:25.169
the world that, if they fail, the entire western
economic system collapses that you have no

00:23:25.169 --> 00:23:28.210
choice but to bail them out, unpopular as
that was.

00:23:28.210 --> 00:23:31.419
If you don't bail them out we all go back
to living in conditions that were before the

00:23:31.419 --> 00:23:35.230
Great Depression, which includes some people
in this room who are reasonably well off,

00:23:35.230 --> 00:23:37.040
as happened in the Great Depression.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:42.360
So you either have to have a lot of regulation,
which I'm not that crazy about, or you have

00:23:42.360 --> 00:23:46.470
to have rules in capitalism which let capitalism
really work.

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:49.269
And capitalism hasn't worked as well as it
could have.

00:23:49.269 --> 00:23:54.650
It has worked well enough to lift this country
to the—the highest standard of living, essentially,

00:23:54.650 --> 00:23:58.620
in the world, if you adjust for demography
and so forth and so on.

00:23:58.620 --> 00:24:02.840
But it hasn't worked well enough so that we've
taken the kinks out of it, so there can be

00:24:02.840 --> 00:24:04.300
adequate distribution of wealth.

00:24:04.300 --> 00:24:08.500
We're not talking about socialism or making
sure everybody pays a 90 percent tax rate,

00:24:08.500 --> 00:24:10.640
as they did when LBJ was president.

00:24:10.640 --> 00:24:12.340
We know that doesn't work.

00:24:12.340 --> 00:24:18.009
But what I do believe is that in a capitalist
society there have to be rules so that everybody

00:24:18.009 --> 00:24:22.409
has some skin in the game and some investment
in making capitalism succeed and that's the

00:24:22.409 --> 00:24:23.730
danger I see in this country.

00:24:23.730 --> 00:24:24.730
HIRSH: Grover.

00:24:24.730 --> 00:24:25.730
NORQUIST: Yeah.

00:24:25.730 --> 00:24:29.759
You sound, in defending these programs, that
we're going to fix them, like my Republican

00:24:29.759 --> 00:24:37.960
friends who say "The Iraq war wasn't a mistake,
but we're never doing that again'" And I think

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:44.690
you have to go back and look at the damage
that these programs did.

00:24:44.690 --> 00:24:47.980
They made things worse than they would have
been, OK?

00:24:47.980 --> 00:24:53.260
They took half of the government over—today.

00:24:53.260 --> 00:24:56.279
Social Security, for instance, if—this was
not an idea out of Kansas.

00:24:56.279 --> 00:24:58.200
It's an idea out of Germany.

00:24:58.200 --> 00:25:05.480
Social Security was thought up by our friend
who unified the German state.

00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:12.780
And the whole—his project, it doesn't transfer
well over here.

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:16.529
Social Security isn't an investment program.

00:25:16.529 --> 00:25:21.390
We're now, today, in the states and private
sector's almost all shifted over, with the

00:25:21.390 --> 00:25:25.600
exception of some unionized companies, from
defined benefit plans to defined contribution

00:25:25.600 --> 00:25:29.000
plans, 401ks, IRAs.

00:25:29.000 --> 00:25:33.100
And if you don't have that you do end up with
people making all sorts of commitments into

00:25:33.100 --> 00:25:38.019
the future and the workers get screwed and
the companies go bankrupt and consumers get

00:25:38.019 --> 00:25:39.019
cheated.

00:25:39.019 --> 00:25:41.450
And the private sector has largely shifted
that over.

00:25:41.450 --> 00:25:46.950
We're—now we're going state and moving from
defined benefit plans, where politicians would

00:25:46.950 --> 00:25:51.659
promise you the world today, because by the
time you collect an uncollectable and unpayable

00:25:51.659 --> 00:25:56.230
pension, because it's promising more than
the government's willing to take, I'll be

00:25:56.230 --> 00:26:00.120
dead or out of office and it's somebody else's
problem.

00:26:00.120 --> 00:26:04.190
So states—Utah's completely shifted over
to defined contribution.

00:26:04.190 --> 00:26:09.950
A number of other states, Michigan, at the
state level, completely shifted over back

00:26:09.950 --> 00:26:15.789
in 1990 and more and more states are moving
in that direction.

00:26:15.789 --> 00:26:23.020
Had we done Social Security that way, we wouldn't
have a $10 trillion unfunded liability.

00:26:23.020 --> 00:26:28.700
If you fixed—Ryan and—Congressman Ryan
and Senator Sununu put forward a plan that

00:26:28.700 --> 00:26:32.809
the Social Security actuary went and rated.

00:26:32.809 --> 00:26:37.000
And he said look, if you'd phased in allowing
you to take your FICA taxes and putting them

00:26:37.000 --> 00:26:42.150
in a 401k that you control and people over
fifty-five stay in the old system, everybody

00:26:42.150 --> 00:26:48.570
else moves over to the new one, by 2024, we'd
have $7 trillion in those accounts.

00:26:48.570 --> 00:26:52.460
Why do we have inequality of wealth in this
country?

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:58.670
Because we take the income that low-income
people and young people make and we take it

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:03.799
in Social Security taxes and we tell you it's
savings, but it's not.

00:27:03.799 --> 00:27:06.960
If it was savings, low-income people would
actually have savings.

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:09.600
They'd have a resource to share with their
family.

00:27:09.600 --> 00:27:16.350
They would be able to handle those and $7
trillion, fairly evenly spread out across

00:27:16.350 --> 00:27:22.630
the country, so the Social Security that everybody's
in, would dramatically change the inequality

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:28.649
that, in terms of wealth accumulation in this
country, Social Security created and made

00:27:28.649 --> 00:27:34.000
possible and made it difficult for people
to save for their future, particularly—I

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:39.399
mean, rich people can save for their future,
but low-income people find that Social Security

00:27:39.399 --> 00:27:41.559
takes their money and they can't.

00:27:41.559 --> 00:27:46.429
And by the way, you could not only do that,
but drop the FICA tax from what would be 20

00:27:46.429 --> 00:27:51.559
percent if nothing was done, down to five
percent in order to deal with disability and

00:27:51.559 --> 00:27:55.960
some of the non-saving portions of Social
Security.

00:27:55.960 --> 00:28:02.309
If we look at Medicaid, if we block-granted
that to the states, as we have done with Aid

00:28:02.309 --> 00:28:06.159
to Families of Dependent Children, I know—under
Clinton, Clinton did this.

00:28:06.159 --> 00:28:08.470
I know that the left says the world would
end.

00:28:08.470 --> 00:28:15.030
Well, they all said that when—when Clinton
signed the third bill that the Republicans

00:28:15.030 --> 00:28:21.539
passed to move Aid to Families with Dependent
Children, now TANF, out to the states, so

00:28:21.539 --> 00:28:25.190
you have fifty states trying fifty different
things.

00:28:25.190 --> 00:28:30.519
What—what our friends at the state have
missed is that one of the reasons governments

00:28:30.519 --> 00:28:37.299
don't do very—governments can blow things
up, but they're not very good at creating

00:28:37.299 --> 00:28:38.950
wealth.

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:44.299
They—or creating new innovations going into
the future.

00:28:44.299 --> 00:28:47.730
And the reason is, by definition, they're
a monopoly.

00:28:47.730 --> 00:28:52.379
The advantage of states over the federal government,it's
not this 'closer to the people' stuff.

00:28:52.379 --> 00:28:56.740
I'm not any closer to the mayor of D.C. than
I am to the president of the United States,

00:28:56.740 --> 00:29:00.460
in terms of being able to call him on the
phone and suggest something.

00:29:00.460 --> 00:29:07.320
But there are fifty states—some say fifty-seven,
but at least fifty and they can take different

00:29:07.320 --> 00:29:11.950
approaches and this is why I think it's very
important that we allow the fifty states to

00:29:11.950 --> 00:29:14.990
take different approaches and let's find out
what works.

00:29:14.990 --> 00:29:20.710
And I think it's obvious that defined contribution
pensions are better than defined benefit pensions,

00:29:20.710 --> 00:29:25.980
in terms of being stable and creating wealth
for all Americans, not just the Kennedys and

00:29:25.980 --> 00:29:27.779
the Rockefellers.

00:29:27.779 --> 00:29:30.110
And that's the missed opportunity.

00:29:30.110 --> 00:29:37.049
The number of heads of households, people
who are out of work because of these programs

00:29:37.049 --> 00:29:38.649
are devastating there.

00:29:38.649 --> 00:29:43.210
But what Social Security has done in replacing
savings and telling poor and middle-income

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:46.880
people you can't save because we're taking
your money, promising you it's being saved

00:29:46.880 --> 00:29:54.029
in West Virginia somewhere, but it's not,
has created tremendous inequality and the

00:29:54.029 --> 00:29:59.410
fact that we have very bad job creation in
this country.

00:29:59.410 --> 00:30:02.289
That's one of the reasons why low-income people
are not doing better.

00:30:02.289 --> 00:30:04.379
We need to be having more jobs.

00:30:04.379 --> 00:30:06.230
HIRSH: Brief—very briefly, before we go
to...

00:30:06.230 --> 00:30:10.470
DEAN: OK, so here's the central dilemma of
the argument that we're having, what do you

00:30:10.470 --> 00:30:12.580
do about the outliers?

00:30:12.580 --> 00:30:17.600
So in the—in the case of Social Security
and defined contribution, I'm not unwilling

00:30:17.600 --> 00:30:23.240
to look at that, but the problem is what do
you do about the people who are taken advantage

00:30:23.240 --> 00:30:26.980
of or end up in some investment scheme.

00:30:26.980 --> 00:30:30.660
We know there were a lot of people who lost
their IRAs in—in the 2008 recession, because

00:30:30.660 --> 00:30:34.340
of a lot of the chicanery on Wall Street and
some of it was just bad luck.

00:30:34.340 --> 00:30:38.450
Or maybe they just made bad investment decisions
and they don't have an IRA.

00:30:38.450 --> 00:30:41.361
What do they retire on if there's nothing
to fall back on?

00:30:41.361 --> 00:30:43.820
And what is our obligation to keep them from
starving?

00:30:43.820 --> 00:30:45.730
Do we have one?

00:30:45.730 --> 00:30:48.750
Same is true of states.

00:30:48.750 --> 00:30:50.500
Texas is my—my favorite example.

00:30:50.500 --> 00:30:54.259
Lot of people moving into Texas, Texas' economy
doing really great.

00:30:54.259 --> 00:30:58.200
Twenty-two percent of the kids, in spite of
CHIP, have no health insurance in the state

00:30:58.200 --> 00:30:59.870
of Texas, 22 percent.

00:30:59.870 --> 00:31:02.500
Now those are American kids, not just Texan
kids.

00:31:02.500 --> 00:31:05.480
I think I have a responsibility for those
kids as an American.

00:31:05.480 --> 00:31:09.690
So the question is, in this, in this area
where every—everything has devolved and

00:31:09.690 --> 00:31:14.440
everything is—is let's let the states, what
about the people who screw up and what about

00:31:14.440 --> 00:31:16.070
the kids who don't get health insurance?

00:31:16.070 --> 00:31:19.240
What obligation do we have as a nation to
those children?

00:31:19.240 --> 00:31:21.559
So I think it's easy to say oh, well let's
decentralize.

00:31:21.559 --> 00:31:23.210
Let's have defined contributions.

00:31:23.210 --> 00:31:26.509
There are situations we have to talk about
and you have to deal with.

00:31:26.509 --> 00:31:29.559
We have to define our responsibility for each
other.

00:31:29.559 --> 00:31:30.559
What is it?

00:31:30.559 --> 00:31:31.559
Do we have any?

00:31:31.559 --> 00:31:32.889
Because I think if we don't, we're not a nation.

00:31:32.889 --> 00:31:34.270
We're certainly not going to be a great nation.

00:31:34.270 --> 00:31:39.110
The last point I want to make is I would disagree
with the idea that we don't foster innovation

00:31:39.110 --> 00:31:40.110
or anything.

00:31:40.110 --> 00:31:42.570
You know how we foster innovation and wealth
creation in this country?

00:31:42.570 --> 00:31:45.210
I agree, the government doesn't do it.

00:31:45.210 --> 00:31:47.590
No, I don't agree with that.

00:31:47.590 --> 00:31:54.020
We do it because we provide the absolutely
essential creative framework, which is the

00:31:54.020 --> 00:31:55.490
rule of law.

00:31:55.490 --> 00:31:59.070
That is the great genius of American and the
great genius of our constitution.

00:31:59.070 --> 00:32:00.759
We don't do the innovation.

00:32:00.759 --> 00:32:08.029
We set a—a—we set forth a table that millions
and millions of people come to this country

00:32:08.029 --> 00:32:10.889
to do their innovation, because we have a
predictable rule of law.

00:32:10.889 --> 00:32:12.820
And that, we need to focus on.

00:32:12.820 --> 00:32:17.629
What the rule of law says is that the table
is level for everybody, whether you're rich

00:32:17.629 --> 00:32:22.940
or poor or in between, no matter what your
circumstances are, if you're willing to work

00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:26.549
hard and play by the rules, you can make a
lot of money and do well.

00:32:26.549 --> 00:32:32.429
If we lose that and if we lose people believing
that, then that's when we're in trouble.

00:32:32.429 --> 00:32:33.899
Right now, I don't think we're in trouble.

00:32:33.899 --> 00:32:35.220
We've got some things to do.

00:32:35.220 --> 00:32:36.879
Financially, we've got a lot of work to do.

00:32:36.879 --> 00:32:38.629
We got to make some tough decisions.

00:32:38.629 --> 00:32:41.179
I think the fundamental nature of this country
is still sound.

00:32:41.179 --> 00:32:44.980
NORQUIST: But I think in the spirit—in the
spirit of bipartisanship, I agree there's

00:32:44.980 --> 00:32:50.509
a role for the government in providing rule
of law and protecting property rights and

00:32:50.509 --> 00:32:54.950
right of contracts so that people don't cheat
each other.

00:32:54.950 --> 00:32:57.950
HIRSH: Let's open it up to Q&amp;A.

00:32:57.950 --> 00:33:03.309
Please wait for the microphone to come to
you and stand and state your name and affiliation

00:33:03.309 --> 00:33:05.460
and try to keep things as concise as possible.

00:33:05.460 --> 00:33:07.159
We have less than a half an hour for this.

00:33:07.159 --> 00:33:09.090
Yes, sir, right over here.

00:33:09.090 --> 00:33:11.249
QUESTION: Chris Broughton, Millennium Challenge
Corporation.

00:33:11.249 --> 00:33:13.919
Great discussion, gentlemen, thank you for
being with us here today.

00:33:13.919 --> 00:33:18.379
First, I want to start with a quote of Paul—by
Paul Krugman, "the United States government

00:33:18.379 --> 00:33:22.090
is turning into an insurance company with
a standing army".

00:33:22.090 --> 00:33:25.010
The second piece is really political.

00:33:25.010 --> 00:33:29.529
It's the political challenge that we—don't
think we delved into enough about tackling

00:33:29.529 --> 00:33:35.340
entitlements, which both of you I think recognize
the fiscal threat to you and it's really the

00:33:35.340 --> 00:33:40.350
NIMBY problem, which is general used in environmental
terms, but not—"not in my benefit yo", you

00:33:40.350 --> 00:33:42.590
might say in the entitlements context.

00:33:42.590 --> 00:33:47.549
So the first piece of that is there've been
lots of proposal, Bowles-Simpson, Rivlin,

00:33:47.549 --> 00:33:48.549
et cetera.

00:33:48.549 --> 00:33:50.509
None of them have moved forward.

00:33:50.509 --> 00:33:54.520
There's also the element that when we think
about those entitlements, it's actually a

00:33:54.520 --> 00:33:56.919
resource transfer from blue states to red
states.

00:33:56.919 --> 00:33:58.039
I'm getting to my question.

00:33:58.039 --> 00:33:59.039
I'm going quick.

00:33:59.039 --> 00:34:00.039
HIRSH: Good.

00:34:00.039 --> 00:34:01.970
QUESTION: So the first is how do you tackle
the entitlement problem?

00:34:01.970 --> 00:34:03.470
And it's both a red state and a blue state.

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:04.490
Everyone benefits, right?

00:34:04.490 --> 00:34:07.820
And - and—and the second piece of it is
it's also structural to the United States

00:34:07.820 --> 00:34:08.820
economy, right?

00:34:08.820 --> 00:34:13.210
So, Grover, you were talking about the comparison
to the Great Society and conditions then and

00:34:13.210 --> 00:34:15.579
now and—and—and the role of entitlements
in that.

00:34:15.579 --> 00:34:19.409
But one could argue it's actually more just
the structure of the economy, globalization,

00:34:19.409 --> 00:34:24.180
mechanization, shift from manufacturing services,
has far more to do than—than the entitlement

00:34:24.180 --> 00:34:27.010
programs in terms of the structure of jobs
and employment and so forth.

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:29.790
So how do you think about those, both the
political problem and also the structure of

00:34:29.790 --> 00:34:30.790
the economy?

00:34:30.790 --> 00:34:32.360
Thank you.

00:34:32.360 --> 00:34:39.870
NORQUIST: Look, you—when you said...I would
argue that these programs have made the situation

00:34:39.870 --> 00:34:46.760
worse, not better, because there were alternatives—ways
to spend those resources.

00:34:46.760 --> 00:34:52.099
It could have been done, in some cases, with
fifty states competing to do it differently.

00:34:52.099 --> 00:34:57.819
I realize that the modern Democratic Party
does not like the idea of allowing states

00:34:57.819 --> 00:35:04.390
to make these decisions, because, as you look
at it, there are twenty-four states with a

00:35:04.390 --> 00:35:06.920
Republican governor, Republican House and
Republican Senate.

00:35:06.920 --> 00:35:10.950
This is shortly after the election, when the
New York Times announced the modern Republican

00:35:10.950 --> 00:35:13.099
Party had ceased to exist in the world.

00:35:13.099 --> 00:35:17.000
And there are now seven states that Democrats
run completely.

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:23.130
Half the country lives under a Republican
governance and one-third of that—one-sixth

00:35:23.130 --> 00:35:25.060
lives in—under Democratic governance.

00:35:25.060 --> 00:35:30.030
If you look at the blue—the blue states,
their statespeople tend to leave and the red

00:35:30.030 --> 00:35:33.380
states, their statespeople either have been
moving, too, for a while or continuing to

00:35:33.380 --> 00:35:35.670
move to.

00:35:35.670 --> 00:35:38.599
So we—we can tell what works and what doesn't.

00:35:38.599 --> 00:35:44.650
That's why I suggest that when we discuss
what's going to work and what doesn't, we

00:35:44.650 --> 00:35:51.040
take a look in two years and four years and
six years, because these red states are staying

00:35:51.040 --> 00:35:54.990
red for the rest of the decade and the blue
states staying blue for the rest of the decade

00:35:54.990 --> 00:35:56.140
and maybe another ten years.

00:35:56.140 --> 00:35:59.670
And we will actually have a—a controlled
experiment.

00:35:59.670 --> 00:36:05.290
Had this conversation with a nice lady who
runs the Nation magazine and she turned green,

00:36:05.290 --> 00:36:10.869
because she doesn't think that if you take
her ideas and you do them in a state it'll

00:36:10.869 --> 00:36:11.869
work.

00:36:11.869 --> 00:36:14.619
Really stupid ideas can only be done at the
national level.

00:36:14.619 --> 00:36:15.619
If you have...

00:36:15.619 --> 00:36:16.619
(LAUGHTER)

00:36:16.619 --> 00:36:19.210
... you have a really stupid idea and you
do it in Massachusetts, like government-run

00:36:19.210 --> 00:36:22.920
healthcare under Dukakis and then the repealed
it as soon as he lost the presidential election,

00:36:22.920 --> 00:36:24.140
because it was just for show.

00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:26.280
We're not going to live under this.

00:36:26.280 --> 00:36:27.349
You can't do that in a state.

00:36:27.349 --> 00:36:31.780
Vermont just went through a similar conversation
about government-controlled healthcare.

00:36:31.780 --> 00:36:38.130
So there's a reason why fifty states are better
than a federal government for looking at these

00:36:38.130 --> 00:36:39.320
sorts of projects.

00:36:39.320 --> 00:36:43.700
And even better is 300 million Americans minding
their own business, doing their own stuff.

00:36:43.700 --> 00:36:46.240
HIRSH: Thank you, Mr. Jefferson.

00:36:46.240 --> 00:36:47.240
Yes.

00:36:47.240 --> 00:36:48.240
DEAN: I—I—I...

00:36:48.240 --> 00:36:52.790
NORQUIST: There's nothing old about the idea
of the Constitution.

00:36:52.790 --> 00:36:58.700
DEAN: I do like the quote from Paul Krugman,
which I hadn't heard, even though it is a

00:36:58.700 --> 00:37:04.020
bit of a unrelated, it reminds me of John
McCain's quote about Russia, a—"a gas station

00:37:04.020 --> 00:37:06.440
posing as a country."

00:37:06.440 --> 00:37:10.670
I mean, I—I don't like talking in sound
bites and I really don't like Washington talking

00:37:10.670 --> 00:37:16.180
point speaking-ese, but when you get a really
good one, you just got to repeat it.

00:37:16.180 --> 00:37:17.180
(LAUGHTER)

00:37:17.180 --> 00:37:24.890
Look, I think we do need changes in entitlements.

00:37:24.890 --> 00:37:29.130
And the question is how can we make this—this—these
programs survive?

00:37:29.130 --> 00:37:35.310
I've already talked about why I'm not supportive
particularly of giving the states more flexibility.

00:37:35.310 --> 00:37:36.599
I was when I was governor.

00:37:36.599 --> 00:37:41.250
I was the head of the National Governor's
Association, but I've since—Texas has really

00:37:41.250 --> 00:37:43.119
just thrown me for a loop.

00:37:43.119 --> 00:37:47.410
Here's a state with rising economy that's
bragged about by its governor, which not only

00:37:47.410 --> 00:37:51.450
has 22 percent of all its children with no
health insurance, they're 48th in the country

00:37:51.450 --> 00:37:54.250
in terms of educational achievement in high
school.

00:37:54.250 --> 00:37:58.700
I mean I—I—I don't—we can't risk having
the third—second—third largest state in

00:37:58.700 --> 00:38:01.569
the country—second largest state in the
country, population-wise.

00:38:01.569 --> 00:38:08.040
We can't risk having that many people get
out of high school who are ignorant.

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:14.030
And so just think there is a federal interest
in making sure there's a minimum level of—I

00:38:14.030 --> 00:38:15.030
support the common core.

00:38:15.030 --> 00:38:18.319
I don't—I have some problems with some of
the testing.

00:38:18.319 --> 00:38:19.790
Maybe we can look at that.

00:38:19.790 --> 00:38:24.119
The idea that nine times nine is something
other than eighty-one in states if they choose

00:38:24.119 --> 00:38:26.250
not to test for it is insane.

00:38:26.250 --> 00:38:30.260
Mississippi used to dumb down their tests
to make sure that an adequate number of people

00:38:30.260 --> 00:38:32.750
passed it to satisfy the Department of Education.

00:38:32.750 --> 00:38:33.859
This is crazy.

00:38:33.859 --> 00:38:37.609
We are one country and if we're not one big
country, we're not going to be the most powerful

00:38:37.609 --> 00:38:39.099
country in the face of the Earth.

00:38:39.099 --> 00:38:43.540
Part of the reason we're as powerful as we
are is because we have 310 million people

00:38:43.540 --> 00:38:47.829
that can generate wealth for common cause
among the fifty states.

00:38:47.829 --> 00:38:53.000
So I—I just think this notion—I'm very
willing to look at the federal programs and

00:38:53.000 --> 00:38:54.040
see if they can't be changed.

00:38:54.040 --> 00:38:55.980
I supported the sequester.

00:38:55.980 --> 00:39:00.240
The reason I supported the sequester was because
I thought that no way was anybody ever going

00:39:00.240 --> 00:39:03.160
to make those cuts if they had to vote on
them.

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:05.540
I'm serious about budget cutting.

00:39:05.540 --> 00:39:09.780
But I'm not serious if we can all punt and
hope that somebody at the local level—what

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:13.319
makes you think that the local government
is any more effective than federal government?

00:39:13.319 --> 00:39:17.170
We get madder at the federal government, but,
you know, I've seen the legislature in some

00:39:17.170 --> 00:39:18.170
of these places.

00:39:18.170 --> 00:39:19.170
NORQUIST: Because—because you can leave.

00:39:19.170 --> 00:39:20.430
HIRSH: Yeah, there's an argument for accountability
there.

00:39:20.430 --> 00:39:21.430
Let's—let's go to...

00:39:21.430 --> 00:39:22.430
NORQUIST: I'm sorry, one second.

00:39:22.430 --> 00:39:23.430
Krugman was wrong.

00:39:23.430 --> 00:39:26.510
I—I—I really apologize for not pointing
out...when I quoted Krugman, I failed to point

00:39:26.510 --> 00:39:27.819
out that he was wrong.

00:39:27.819 --> 00:39:31.790
The fed—the federal government is a Ponzi
scheme for the standing army, not an insurance

00:39:31.790 --> 00:39:32.790
company.

00:39:32.790 --> 00:39:33.790
HIRSH: OK, very good.

00:39:33.790 --> 00:39:34.790
Let's go to...

00:39:34.790 --> 00:39:35.790
(LAUGHTER)

00:39:35.790 --> 00:39:36.790
Let's go to a question over here, Mike Haltzel.

00:39:36.790 --> 00:39:37.790
Please be succinct.

00:39:37.790 --> 00:39:39.630
QUESTION: Yeah, thank you.

00:39:39.630 --> 00:39:41.310
I'm Mike Haltzel from Johns Hopkins.

00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:42.630
Guys, terrific discussion.

00:39:42.630 --> 00:39:47.020
Look, the one thing we haven't—that you
folks really haven't done very much is put

00:39:47.020 --> 00:39:49.530
the foreign in the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:39:49.530 --> 00:39:54.130
I'd—unless we think we live on an island
and other people's experiences are completely

00:39:54.130 --> 00:39:57.990
irrelevant, and I don't think either one of
you think so, I think we have to look at it.

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:04.240
I love my country, Mr. Dean, as much as anybody
and we're successful in many ways.

00:40:04.240 --> 00:40:09.329
But we're not so successful in many ways,
whether it's life expectancy or infant mortality

00:40:09.329 --> 00:40:12.890
or—or scores of our—of our students.

00:40:12.890 --> 00:40:16.400
You can argue about how that's—you know,
there are some variables there, but the fact

00:40:16.400 --> 00:40:18.160
is we're not perfect.

00:40:18.160 --> 00:40:22.900
You talk about Social Security, you alluded
to Bismarck and said it's just not transferrable.

00:40:22.900 --> 00:40:29.430
It—it strikes me, we're—the last time
I looked at OECD statistics, we were the third-lowest

00:40:29.430 --> 00:40:34.210
taxed of all the thirty-four—I think Mexico
and Chile were taxed lower than we were.

00:40:34.210 --> 00:40:39.380
So the vast majority are taxed more and yet
these countries that tax more and presumably

00:40:39.380 --> 00:40:45.820
redistribute more have better—I'll use the
value word, distribution of wealth than we

00:40:45.820 --> 00:40:47.960
do, yet you're saying we ought to go the other
direction.

00:40:47.960 --> 00:40:49.460
I just don't understand that.

00:40:49.460 --> 00:40:54.900
And—and so, my question—I'll just stop
and say could you both maybe discuss a little

00:40:54.900 --> 00:40:56.819
bit about international comparisons?

00:40:56.819 --> 00:41:01.210
And I'm saying this, I—I know at a time
when Europe's having problems, so I lead with

00:41:01.210 --> 00:41:03.290
my chin, but that's largely because of...

00:41:03.290 --> 00:41:07.080
HIRSH: Let's—let's go to that, yeah, particularly
since the President, in his speech last night,

00:41:07.080 --> 00:41:12.220
I think made a big point of saying, you know,
we're—we may not be great, but we're doing

00:41:12.220 --> 00:41:13.300
better than everyone else.

00:41:13.300 --> 00:41:17.329
NORQUIST: Yeah, look, our government sucks
less than all the others.

00:41:17.329 --> 00:41:18.329
(LAUGHTER)

00:41:18.329 --> 00:41:19.920
And I think we should be proud of that.

00:41:19.920 --> 00:41:20.920
HIRSH: End the discussion right.

00:41:20.920 --> 00:41:21.920
NORQUIST: Our government...

00:41:21.920 --> 00:41:22.920
(LAUGHTER)

00:41:22.920 --> 00:41:27.849
It is a matter of patriotic honesty to point
that this government, for all its failures,

00:41:27.849 --> 00:41:32.150
is less horrible and is less destructive of
economic growth and human happiness than most

00:41:32.150 --> 00:41:36.400
other governments you can think of in the
world throughout history, if not all, OK.

00:41:36.400 --> 00:41:38.980
And the problem is it could be all net positive.

00:41:38.980 --> 00:41:42.060
It doesn't have to have all of the negatives.

00:41:42.060 --> 00:41:43.069
We've made some real positive.

00:41:43.069 --> 00:41:47.430
We've cut but we have a lot more to do.

00:41:47.430 --> 00:41:51.839
Europe is saddled with a VAT, which is the
difference between the United States—Europe

00:41:51.839 --> 00:41:53.319
is the United States plus a VAT.

00:41:53.319 --> 00:41:58.860
Each country is about that much bigger a state
than ours.

00:41:58.860 --> 00:42:01.609
People—trust people.

00:42:01.609 --> 00:42:05.070
People move to Texas, oh, Texas is no good.

00:42:05.070 --> 00:42:12.450
Well, you can take a look at it if you—not
mentioned is Texas is a high immigrant state.

00:42:12.450 --> 00:42:17.150
Immigrants in Texas test better than immigrants
in other places, as does everybody else.

00:42:17.150 --> 00:42:20.380
But they have more of the immigrants and they
don't test as well as other guys.

00:42:20.380 --> 00:42:24.730
But if you're doing apples to apples on immigrants
and so on, you are getting—Texas does a

00:42:24.730 --> 00:42:26.730
better job of educating them than other states.

00:42:26.730 --> 00:42:35.329
If you're from really close to the Canadian
border, as Moynihan pointed out, things can

00:42:35.329 --> 00:42:36.329
be different.

00:42:36.329 --> 00:42:40.140
But if you want to do apples to apples on—on
how well states are doing, the fact that people

00:42:40.140 --> 00:42:47.330
move there from other states tells you something
about how—what people thinks works and what

00:42:47.330 --> 00:42:48.330
it doesn't.

00:42:48.330 --> 00:42:49.950
Same thing with people coming to the United
States.

00:42:49.950 --> 00:42:52.020
Europe does not do immigration very well.

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:54.160
That's why they're the past and we're the
future.

00:42:54.160 --> 00:42:57.100
China and Japan don't do immigration well.

00:42:57.100 --> 00:43:03.020
They're going to have real problems maintaining
the sort of social welfare transfers as they

00:43:03.020 --> 00:43:09.540
get older, before they get rich, as they have
declining—continuing declining labor forces

00:43:09.540 --> 00:43:12.460
and ours can be expanding as much as we want
to.

00:43:12.460 --> 00:43:15.089
HIRSH: So let—Governor—Governor, do you—do—do
you agree that...

00:43:15.089 --> 00:43:18.000
DEAN: I think it's—I think it's—the point
is well taken.

00:43:18.000 --> 00:43:21.829
I deeply appreciate that question.

00:43:21.829 --> 00:43:25.520
Let's look at what we do well and what the
Europeans do well.

00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:31.620
They have a much higher index of—of wealth
equality, if you'll say that.

00:43:31.620 --> 00:43:36.559
And it is borne out of the fact that education
levels and borne out of infant mortality.

00:43:36.559 --> 00:43:41.570
It's even borne out of happiness measure,
however that—even I will admit that's a

00:43:41.570 --> 00:43:45.650
little squishy soft social science.

00:43:45.650 --> 00:43:48.790
And they have more redistribution of wealth.

00:43:48.790 --> 00:43:51.319
So they do some things very well and some
things not so well.

00:43:51.319 --> 00:43:53.290
I think this is a much more dynamic economy.

00:43:53.290 --> 00:43:54.920
The Europeans know that.

00:43:54.920 --> 00:43:56.589
I'm fascinated by the German economy.

00:43:56.589 --> 00:44:00.200
You know, we have labor problems here from
time to time.

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:03.270
France has labor problems here from time to
time.

00:44:03.270 --> 00:44:05.099
Germany mostly doesn't.

00:44:05.099 --> 00:44:06.349
Why?

00:44:06.349 --> 00:44:13.430
Because they have a model for integrating
unions and investor—and investors in corporations,

00:44:13.430 --> 00:44:16.290
which I think is the envy of the world and
we ought to adopt it here.

00:44:16.290 --> 00:44:19.030
We can learn from Europe: Europe can lean
from us.

00:44:19.030 --> 00:44:23.620
I was in Europe not long ago and I was scheduled
to come home on a Lufthansa flight and I was

00:44:23.620 --> 00:44:27.130
told, "oh my God, there's going to be a Lufthansa
strike in the next days" and I was leaving

00:44:27.130 --> 00:44:28.130
the day after, so I'm in a panic.

00:44:28.130 --> 00:44:30.650
I don't know what to do.

00:44:30.650 --> 00:44:32.690
I'm sure the airport is going to be a disaster.

00:44:32.690 --> 00:44:37.500
Oh no, they only go on strike for one day,
because they—when they get serious in the

00:44:37.500 --> 00:44:41.619
labor negotiations, that's how they let the
management know they're very upset.

00:44:41.619 --> 00:44:43.730
I mean, so no productivity was lost.

00:44:43.730 --> 00:44:47.390
Lufthansa didn't use—lose a bundle of money
and the labor unions realized that that was

00:44:47.390 --> 00:44:51.049
to their advantage too, because they could
get more if the company did better.

00:44:51.049 --> 00:44:54.960
I mean we need to start looking at European
models.

00:44:54.960 --> 00:45:01.160
The—I—one of the fool—most foolish things
Mitt Romney said last timein a long list,

00:45:01.160 --> 00:45:04.020
was that—was we don't want to be like Europe.

00:45:04.020 --> 00:45:05.980
We don't want to be like Europe and we're
not Europe.

00:45:05.980 --> 00:45:10.440
And I absolutely agree that they have done
a lousy job integrating immigrants and—and

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:12.320
they're paying a huge price for that now.

00:45:12.320 --> 00:45:13.320
And we struggle with that.

00:45:13.320 --> 00:45:16.569
We do better than anybody else in—on the
face of the earth.

00:45:16.569 --> 00:45:20.080
But there are some things over there—we
ought not to blanketly dismiss everything

00:45:20.080 --> 00:45:23.200
they do in Europe, because there are some
things they do, particularly the things that

00:45:23.200 --> 00:45:28.069
result in the indicators that you just talked
about, and labor relations, which we could

00:45:28.069 --> 00:45:32.619
learn a lot from and we should learn a lot
from, because it would make our economy stronger.

00:45:32.619 --> 00:45:37.183
HIRSH: Let's go back to this side of the room,
if there are any questions on this side of

00:45:37.183 --> 00:45:38.183
the room.

00:45:38.183 --> 00:45:39.740
Gentleman right over there.

00:45:39.740 --> 00:45:42.180
QUESTION: Yeah, Ted Alden from the Council
on Foreign Relations.

00:45:42.180 --> 00:45:45.950
Wanted to push a little harder on the defined
contribution idea, because I think in—in

00:45:45.950 --> 00:45:48.349
the private sector, in a lot of ways it's
worked well.

00:45:48.349 --> 00:45:50.849
It's better for the companies in terms of
cost control.

00:45:50.849 --> 00:45:54.180
For people who are paying into the plans,
market returns have been pretty good.

00:45:54.180 --> 00:45:57.350
But we know that most people are not saving
nearly enough for retirement: certainly not

00:45:57.350 --> 00:46:02.010
putting aside enough money to ensure any kind
of security in retirement.

00:46:02.010 --> 00:46:04.030
Why is that going to work better on the Social
Security side?

00:46:04.030 --> 00:46:06.400
Are you—are you talking about a sort of
mandated contribution?

00:46:06.400 --> 00:46:08.890
Everybody's going to have to do this and it's
going to be managed privately.

00:46:08.890 --> 00:46:12.540
How do you prevent people from not looking
after their own futures, in effect, in the

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:13.620
way they ought to.

00:46:13.620 --> 00:46:18.020
NORQUIST: Yeah, I think when—that all the
plans that are talking about reforming Social

00:46:18.020 --> 00:46:22.040
Security along the Chilean model or some others,
would take the present amount of money the

00:46:22.040 --> 00:46:27.930
government takes from you and gives to somebody
in Wyoming next week and instead requires

00:46:27.930 --> 00:46:31.790
you to take that same amount of money and
put it into a 401k for yourself.

00:46:31.790 --> 00:46:35.130
So the reason people don't save enough is
that the—the government lies to you and

00:46:35.130 --> 00:46:37.950
tells you it's doing the saving for you.

00:46:37.950 --> 00:46:44.140
And so that kind of Ponzi scheme displaces
real savings and so I think that's one of

00:46:44.140 --> 00:46:46.300
the great things that we could get the government
to stop doing.

00:46:46.300 --> 00:46:47.390
It's not a good idea for the government to
lie to people.

00:46:47.390 --> 00:46:49.220
It's not a good idea to steal their money.

00:46:49.220 --> 00:46:52.640
It's not a good idea to tell them there's
money when it's not there.

00:46:52.640 --> 00:46:56.670
The idea that somebody might cheat you, when
the guy talking to you has a $10 trillion

00:46:56.670 --> 00:47:02.390
dollar present value of its unfunded liabilities
or $25 trillion to $35 trillion on Medicare.

00:47:02.390 --> 00:47:03.390
What?

00:47:03.390 --> 00:47:08.180
You're not allowed to be part of the conversation
if you've stolen $10 trillion or $25 trillion,

00:47:08.180 --> 00:47:11.880
$35 trillion from people and told them that
they were going to get it back when they're

00:47:11.880 --> 00:47:15.650
not, under this Ponzi scheme that they've
set up.

00:47:15.650 --> 00:47:19.320
Let's cut our losses on that, take care of
the people that the government lied to.

00:47:19.320 --> 00:47:23.950
Let's us be honest with the older people and
everybody under fifty-five let's move over

00:47:23.950 --> 00:47:25.500
and give them that opportunity.

00:47:25.500 --> 00:47:29.520
And yeah, I would—I do think that it would
pay, at least at the beginning, as you move

00:47:29.520 --> 00:47:32.780
forward, to require at least until they've
saved up to a certain amount of money that

00:47:32.780 --> 00:47:37.420
they could be sure to take care of themselves
in case of an accident or old age that they

00:47:37.420 --> 00:47:38.420
could do that.

00:47:38.420 --> 00:47:39.420
(CROSSTALK)

00:47:39.420 --> 00:47:40.420
NORQUIST: One second.

00:47:40.420 --> 00:47:41.420
When I look at the German workforce, I include
the Turks.

00:47:41.420 --> 00:47:45.280
So people that talk about how happy they are
I think should include everybody in Germany,

00:47:45.280 --> 00:47:46.280
not just some.

00:47:46.280 --> 00:47:49.930
DEAN: So I have a—just a quick question
for you about how you would handle the question

00:47:49.930 --> 00:47:54.950
of people who, for whatever reason, don't
save enough, whether they get screwed by a

00:47:54.950 --> 00:47:59.589
Bernie Madoff or whether they didn't save
enough and it was their own fault.

00:47:59.589 --> 00:48:03.619
So what do you do and what's our responsibility
in a defined contribution program for the

00:48:03.619 --> 00:48:04.619
failures?

00:48:04.619 --> 00:48:06.289
NORQUIST: Well, I think two things.

00:48:06.289 --> 00:48:12.210
It—the—in terms of having a safety net
for somebody gets wiped out by something,

00:48:12.210 --> 00:48:15.730
I think you can look at that when you're talking
about one or two percent of the population

00:48:15.730 --> 00:48:20.650
or a fraction, and somebody could do the calculation
on what percentage of 401ks have turned out

00:48:20.650 --> 00:48:23.390
to be fraudulent.

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:27.390
When you're comparing that with 100 percent
rate of fraud under the government program,

00:48:27.390 --> 00:48:30.900
it seems to me from going from 100 percent
to 1 percent and you go, hey wait, there's

00:48:30.900 --> 00:48:31.900
1 percent here.

00:48:31.900 --> 00:48:34.619
We've been fifty years with 100 percent.

00:48:34.619 --> 00:48:37.771
I think that there should be a little sense
of humility on the part of the government

00:48:37.771 --> 00:48:39.400
who did that.

00:48:39.400 --> 00:48:46.010
And this bifurcation of Vietnam and the Great
Society strikes me as—as a nice trick, but

00:48:46.010 --> 00:48:47.010
it doesn't work.

00:48:47.010 --> 00:48:51.530
The same—you should read that speech that
Johnson gave.

00:48:51.530 --> 00:48:54.950
We're going to have smart people get together
in a room and make these decisions on your

00:48:54.950 --> 00:48:56.200
life and so on.

00:48:56.200 --> 00:48:57.430
That included the draft.

00:48:57.430 --> 00:48:58.430
That included Vietnam.

00:48:58.430 --> 00:48:59.839
It was just the whiz kids.

00:48:59.839 --> 00:49:01.569
They weren't whiz kids who did this.

00:49:01.569 --> 00:49:02.829
The whiz kids did Vietnam.

00:49:02.829 --> 00:49:04.059
You know, I mean the same...

00:49:04.059 --> 00:49:05.059
(CROSSTALK)

00:49:05.059 --> 00:49:06.059
NORQUIST: ...the same government hubris leads
to...

00:49:06.059 --> 00:49:07.059
(CROSSTALK)

00:49:07.059 --> 00:49:08.059
DEAN: OK.

00:49:08.059 --> 00:49:09.059
Let me just insert one...

00:49:09.059 --> 00:49:10.059
(CROSSTALK)

00:49:10.059 --> 00:49:11.059
DEAN: ... one line.

00:49:11.059 --> 00:49:12.059
HIRSH: Very quickly.

00:49:12.059 --> 00:49:13.920
DEAN: All I did was ask one question to get
that response, so I—you know, right now

00:49:13.920 --> 00:49:16.950
the fraud rate in Social Security is zero
percent.

00:49:16.950 --> 00:49:18.980
NORQUIST: From the government.

00:49:18.980 --> 00:49:22.780
They promised there's $10 trillion they've
promised you that isn't there.

00:49:22.780 --> 00:49:23.780
That's the fraud.

00:49:23.780 --> 00:49:26.000
DEAN: So far—so far, every single person
has been paid.

00:49:26.000 --> 00:49:29.070
Now we can argue about what's going to happen
in the future, but to say that Social Security

00:49:29.070 --> 00:49:32.670
is 100 percent fraud is, it's very funny and
amusing but it's certainly not true.

00:49:32.670 --> 00:49:34.420
HIRSH: I'm glad that we brought it back to...

00:49:34.420 --> 00:49:37.100
NORQUIST: The—the—the—the—if—if
the guy—the guy who runs a Ponzi scheme,

00:49:37.100 --> 00:49:40.400
if Madoff had said oh no, I'll get you eventually,
I promise.

00:49:40.400 --> 00:49:42.780
He hadn't cheated anyone until it got caught.

00:49:42.780 --> 00:49:45.510
Everybody'd got paid up to a certain point.

00:49:45.510 --> 00:49:46.599
HIRSH: Government—government as Bernie Madoff.

00:49:46.599 --> 00:49:48.780
Let's go back over here.

00:49:48.780 --> 00:49:52.289
And I—and I'm glad that we brought it back,
Grover, a little bit to the legacy of the

00:49:52.289 --> 00:49:55.270
Great Society itself, because I would like
to try to finish up on that.

00:49:55.270 --> 00:49:56.270
Yes, sir.

00:49:56.270 --> 00:49:57.270
QUESTION: Hi.

00:49:57.270 --> 00:49:58.270
Scott Moore.

00:49:58.270 --> 00:49:59.369
I'm an international affairs fellow here with
the Council.

00:49:59.369 --> 00:50:03.410
So I appreciate that this debate has revolved
quite a bit around evidence.

00:50:03.410 --> 00:50:08.170
I—I feel the need to point out, though,
the case against the Great Society relies

00:50:08.170 --> 00:50:10.630
on trying to prove a counterfactual here.

00:50:10.630 --> 00:50:13.490
We don't know what would have happened without
the Great Society.

00:50:13.490 --> 00:50:17.800
And to return to a previous question, which
I think was very important, if we look around

00:50:17.800 --> 00:50:23.230
the world, we tend to see that some of the
most effective social spending programs are

00:50:23.230 --> 00:50:28.950
forms of conditional cash transfers, which,
if I'm not mistaken, would be typically derided

00:50:28.950 --> 00:50:32.150
here in the United States as handouts.

00:50:32.150 --> 00:50:36.540
So my question is, do we—do we really have
the courage to have this debate about the

00:50:36.540 --> 00:50:38.420
evidence and what works and what doesn't?

00:50:38.420 --> 00:50:39.420
Thank you.

00:50:39.420 --> 00:50:43.150
DEAN: I hope we do and there's a fascinating
article, which I think actually may have been

00:50:43.150 --> 00:50:45.280
in Foreign Affairs, I can't honestly remember.

00:50:45.280 --> 00:50:52.210
But it was about six or eight months ago,
about a project in Kenya, which—in which

00:50:52.210 --> 00:50:59.390
the—the donor organization just gave cash
to people, with no accountability.

00:50:59.390 --> 00:51:04.910
And they did that because our USAID model
is a great organization, but it fundamentally

00:51:04.910 --> 00:51:07.780
makes people more dependent, not less dependent.

00:51:07.780 --> 00:51:09.109
And they just decided to experiment.

00:51:09.109 --> 00:51:14.990
And it turned out that people used it to improve
their lives and their productivity.

00:51:14.990 --> 00:51:19.540
I—they did not go out and spend it on drugs
and gambling and all that good business.

00:51:19.540 --> 00:51:22.740
They went out and put a new roof on their
house or educated their children.

00:51:22.740 --> 00:51:24.119
So it's a fascinating question.

00:51:24.119 --> 00:51:27.480
I think you'd have a really tough time, from
a political point of view, trying to convince

00:51:27.480 --> 00:51:33.810
either American congresspeople or their people
who they represent that this was a great idea

00:51:33.810 --> 00:51:43.020
and we ought to—ought to switch over any
family support to a system like that, because

00:51:43.020 --> 00:51:46.810
I think you'd get the same kind of stuff about
now that you're drug testing and all this

00:51:46.810 --> 00:51:48.000
other business.

00:51:48.000 --> 00:51:51.830
But we ought to be fooling around with ideas
like that and see what works and what doesn't,

00:51:51.830 --> 00:51:55.990
because right now, I would never argue that
we couldn't improve.

00:51:55.990 --> 00:51:59.220
And there is some field work that, in fact,
shows you that outlandish—what appear to

00:51:59.220 --> 00:52:03.069
be outlandish ideas, which I would have thought
was outlandish until I read the research,

00:52:03.069 --> 00:52:06.820
turns out to be not such an outlandish idea
and it's worth pursuing.

00:52:06.820 --> 00:52:11.250
NORQUIST: But remember, one of us up here
says "let's have fifty experiments".

00:52:11.250 --> 00:52:14.700
I'm willing to have real experience and compare.

00:52:14.700 --> 00:52:18.360
The status position of well, we're going to
try things, but the federal government will

00:52:18.360 --> 00:52:22.630
make one big lurch in this direction for fifty
years and that won't work.

00:52:22.630 --> 00:52:24.869
They'll be another big lurch in that direction.

00:52:24.869 --> 00:52:27.349
Governments don't react very rapidly.

00:52:27.349 --> 00:52:30.910
Governments have moved to defined contribution
slowly, OK.

00:52:30.910 --> 00:52:33.261
And that's—it's not a liberal idea, by the
way.

00:52:33.261 --> 00:52:35.710
I mean the—the new lady governor from Rhode
Island, I'm sorry.

00:52:35.710 --> 00:52:41.819
I don't remember her name, but she did significant
reform on their pension, defeated the unions

00:52:41.819 --> 00:52:43.840
in the primary and one in the general.

00:52:43.840 --> 00:52:49.160
San Jose, left of center Mayor, similar reforms.

00:52:49.160 --> 00:52:56.420
OK, this—these New Deal, Great Society programs
are crowding out all the resources that I

00:52:56.420 --> 00:53:00.339
think should be in people's hands and the
liberals think that they'd like to spend,

00:53:00.339 --> 00:53:04.670
but they first have to pry it out of—of—of
those programs.

00:53:04.670 --> 00:53:09.200
I think we ought to have competition among
the ideas and, yes, let's look at Europe.

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:11.950
I mean I think we have a lot to look at Europe.

00:53:11.950 --> 00:53:15.829
When people talk about the United States as
unique, they're usually talking about good

00:53:15.829 --> 00:53:17.060
things, OK.

00:53:17.060 --> 00:53:23.380
Like not having the government run the steel
mills or not having a draft, usually.

00:53:23.380 --> 00:53:27.150
But we're the—we're the country with the
dumbest tort laws in the world and we should

00:53:27.150 --> 00:53:28.650
look at Europe and—and other countries.

00:53:28.650 --> 00:53:30.530
We should look at Sweden for school choice.

00:53:30.530 --> 00:53:36.059
We could look—look at Sweden for their policies
on—on the death tax, like not having one.

00:53:36.059 --> 00:53:38.130
So there are a lot of things that Europe does
well.

00:53:38.130 --> 00:53:40.349
There's a reason it's standing.

00:53:40.349 --> 00:53:43.240
The reason Scandinavian countries are still
there with high tax rates, they do a bunch

00:53:43.240 --> 00:53:45.039
of stuff well.

00:53:45.039 --> 00:53:47.240
We should do that and pass on the things they
do less well.

00:53:47.240 --> 00:53:49.829
HIRSH: Very interesting, some areas of agreement
here.

00:53:49.829 --> 00:53:51.680
Let's take just a couple more.

00:53:51.680 --> 00:53:52.680
Yes, sir.

00:53:52.680 --> 00:53:54.079
Please stand and say your name...

00:53:54.079 --> 00:53:56.359
QUESTION: Nick Eberstadt, American Enterprise
Institute.

00:53:56.359 --> 00:54:01.220
Governor, since the fraud word came up and
since you're a medical doctor, I wanted to

00:54:01.220 --> 00:54:04.450
ask you about the disability programs.

00:54:04.450 --> 00:54:09.910
When you as a doctor see that more people
are getting disability payments than working

00:54:09.910 --> 00:54:14.900
in the manufacturing sector, when you see
that the healthiest generation of American

00:54:14.900 --> 00:54:20.109
workers in history is also the officially
most disabled, how do you respond to that?

00:54:20.109 --> 00:54:21.589
How do you think that through?

00:54:21.589 --> 00:54:23.849
DEAN: I'm—I'm willing to evaluate programs.

00:54:23.849 --> 00:54:28.140
What I'm not willing to do is do what the
Republicans just voted to do in the house,

00:54:28.140 --> 00:54:31.630
which is to blindly cut stuff without knowing
what you're talking about.

00:54:31.630 --> 00:54:36.230
If there's a big disconnect between, OK, there's
problems in the disability program and then,

00:54:36.230 --> 00:54:40.580
OK, so we're not going to transfer $32 billion
to support the program.

00:54:40.580 --> 00:54:44.940
I think it would be nice to actually have
some evidentiary data and some thoughtful

00:54:44.940 --> 00:54:47.569
look at what needs to be done.

00:54:47.569 --> 00:54:51.119
Nobody should be in favor of people getting
something for nothing.

00:54:51.119 --> 00:54:54.619
It's not only bad for the country and the
taxpayers, it's really bad for the people

00:54:54.619 --> 00:54:58.859
who are getting the something-for-nothing,
which is why I was interested in welfare reform.

00:54:58.859 --> 00:55:02.960
On the other hand, I think this blind sort
of attacking this welfare queen pink Cadillac

00:55:02.960 --> 00:55:05.390
stuff is incredibly destructive.

00:55:05.390 --> 00:55:12.380
It's foolish and what it does is put the other
side, which is me, on the defensive so we—we

00:55:12.380 --> 00:55:14.670
instinctively resist all the reforms.

00:55:14.670 --> 00:55:19.609
Why not have a discussion before we start
dropping legislation like that and then try

00:55:19.609 --> 00:55:21.670
to come to some sort of an agreement?

00:55:21.670 --> 00:55:25.480
I think we could agree that there might be
people on disability that don't really need

00:55:25.480 --> 00:55:26.480
to be there.

00:55:26.480 --> 00:55:29.779
I think the solution is probably not cutting
the living hell out of the whole program,

00:55:29.779 --> 00:55:33.549
so that—that—let's just say the 90 percent
of the people who do need to be there are

00:55:33.549 --> 00:55:35.130
getting 18 percent less.

00:55:35.130 --> 00:55:40.700
That's the kind of policy—the problem that
we have in this town and in this country right

00:55:40.700 --> 00:55:44.960
now is that nobody wants to look at the middle
and acknowledge that the other side might

00:55:44.960 --> 00:55:48.930
be right about part of the issue and they
have—do the same for you.

00:55:48.930 --> 00:55:52.400
NORQUIST: I—let me just promise on Boehner's
behalf that if a Democrat comes up with a

00:55:52.400 --> 00:55:57.340
piece of legislation that moves in that direction,
I think we could get a vote on it in the House.

00:55:57.340 --> 00:56:01.319
But when the other team puts out nothing to—and
then criticize the Republicans for not doing

00:56:01.319 --> 00:56:06.329
it differently, I think you then have to say
where is the Democratic Party's willingness

00:56:06.329 --> 00:56:08.190
to have any reforms at all?

00:56:08.190 --> 00:56:15.039
We've had six years without reforms and the
next two years is evidently fantasyland.

00:56:15.039 --> 00:56:19.950
And I think it would pay to have a Democratic
Party that is, at the national level, looking

00:56:19.950 --> 00:56:20.950
at reforms.

00:56:20.950 --> 00:56:25.420
At some states and some local governments,
San Jose, Rhode Island, we're seeing those

00:56:25.420 --> 00:56:27.299
reforms at the state level.

00:56:27.299 --> 00:56:28.299
Why?

00:56:28.299 --> 00:56:31.720
Because states live another—next to other
states and they have to behave or everybody

00:56:31.720 --> 00:56:32.720
does leave.

00:56:32.720 --> 00:56:36.650
HIRSH: Just one more quick question and then
we're going to have to finish up.

00:56:36.650 --> 00:56:38.290
Yes, ma'am.

00:56:38.290 --> 00:56:39.410
QUESTION: Thank you.

00:56:39.410 --> 00:56:41.000
Esther Brimmer now at George Washington University.

00:56:41.000 --> 00:56:45.270
I'd like to go back to the foreign policy
case for actually being one society.

00:56:45.270 --> 00:56:49.559
The real legacy of the Great Society is not
all the—only the specific programs which

00:56:49.559 --> 00:56:50.760
we've been talking about today.

00:56:50.760 --> 00:56:55.420
It's the idea that we are one country and
that we're responsible for ourselves as an

00:56:55.420 --> 00:56:58.900
entire country and that everybody's life in
this country matters.

00:56:58.900 --> 00:57:03.230
So it seem to me when we think about the foreign
policy case that not only do we need, in the

00:57:03.230 --> 00:57:08.059
21st century, a society where no one is hungry,
everyone gets an adequate education because

00:57:08.059 --> 00:57:12.800
innovation comes from everywhere, and that
everybody is able to participate in a—an

00:57:12.800 --> 00:57:17.040
economy which would—gives them an equal
chance to get to the starting line.

00:57:17.040 --> 00:57:18.530
That's what makes us a great country.

00:57:18.530 --> 00:57:22.920
Ultimately, in the 21st century, we will need
not only a great economy and a great military,

00:57:22.920 --> 00:57:27.200
but the power of attraction has made the rest
of the world want to follow the United States.

00:57:27.200 --> 00:57:28.340
That's the legacy of the Great Society.

00:57:28.340 --> 00:57:29.340
Thank you.

00:57:29.340 --> 00:57:30.770
HIRSH: Any—any final thoughts on that?

00:57:30.770 --> 00:57:31.770
NORQUIST: Yeah.

00:57:31.770 --> 00:57:36.350
That's the legacy of America's success is,
the Great Society impeded that, slowed it

00:57:36.350 --> 00:57:38.180
down and damaged it, didn't help.

00:57:38.180 --> 00:57:42.930
We are the country that people want to model
ourselves off and that is, as the governor

00:57:42.930 --> 00:57:48.000
said, providing the rule of law and providing
an opportunity where people are safe.

00:57:48.000 --> 00:57:52.670
Nobody comes and steals your stuff or hits
you on the head and, other than that, leaves

00:57:52.670 --> 00:57:53.950
you alone.

00:57:53.950 --> 00:57:56.770
I worry a little bit about this 'one society'
thing.

00:57:56.770 --> 00:57:57.770
Really?

00:57:57.770 --> 00:58:00.040
What religion are we all going to be?

00:58:00.040 --> 00:58:01.040
You know, what do you mean, one society?

00:58:01.040 --> 00:58:04.309
I—I—when the government decides—again,
read this Lyndon Johnson thing.

00:58:04.309 --> 00:58:05.630
He's going to worry about our soul.

00:58:05.630 --> 00:58:09.549
He's going to worry about our walking out
in the—in—in the fields.

00:58:09.549 --> 00:58:13.369
He's going to worry about our leisure time,
the government's going to get involved it.

00:58:13.369 --> 00:58:19.520
You know, I think the government can and should
protect everybody and—from—from getting

00:58:19.520 --> 00:58:24.279
hurt by somebody else or robbed by somebody
else, protect their house from being broken

00:58:24.279 --> 00:58:26.059
into or—or their car.

00:58:26.059 --> 00:58:31.180
But after that, they should leave people alone
and the challenge is, the government has done

00:58:31.180 --> 00:58:36.920
so many things, it's become damaging to human
program progress, slowing economic growth.

00:58:36.920 --> 00:58:43.920
I mean this is a very, very—I passed this
out, but this is the difference between the

00:58:43.920 --> 00:58:49.559
Reagan growth from when—from depths of the
recession, and the Obama growth from the depths

00:58:49.559 --> 00:58:50.940
of the recession.

00:58:50.940 --> 00:58:52.770
This is 10 million jobs.

00:58:52.770 --> 00:58:56.980
This is a lot of income and wealth that middle-income
people don't have.

00:58:56.980 --> 00:58:59.010
Ten million people are not going to be the
Rockefellers, OK.

00:58:59.010 --> 00:59:02.970
There's going to be 10 million people getting
their first job or getting a job that they

00:59:02.970 --> 00:59:04.369
don't have now.

00:59:04.369 --> 00:59:10.060
The damage done by these bad policies—these
destructive policies compared to Reagan's,

00:59:10.060 --> 00:59:13.270
are 10 million households where dad or mom
doesn't have...

00:59:13.270 --> 00:59:14.270
(CROSSTALK)

00:59:14.270 --> 00:59:15.270
HIRSH: Governor Dean, you have the final word.

00:59:15.270 --> 00:59:16.270
NORQUIST: ... a job.

00:59:16.270 --> 00:59:17.690
HIRSH: Final word, very briefly.

00:59:17.690 --> 00:59:19.180
DEAN: I would agree with Esther.

00:59:19.180 --> 00:59:22.990
I think the key to the Great Society—we
didn't spend a lot of time on this, was in

00:59:22.990 --> 00:59:25.270
fact the notion of getting everybody to the
starting line.

00:59:25.270 --> 00:59:29.799
There was a whole percent of the population
who were not only not at the starting line,

00:59:29.799 --> 00:59:32.150
but they weren't allowed to vote.

00:59:32.150 --> 00:59:35.850
And I think everybody has to fully participate.

00:59:35.850 --> 00:59:39.930
And fully participating, this is where I—this
is where I am not a libertarian.

00:59:39.930 --> 00:59:42.859
Full participation is not 'OK, everybody gets
to vote'.

00:59:42.859 --> 00:59:46.710
Full participation is you have a decent school
system in your neighborhood that's just as

00:59:46.710 --> 00:59:49.750
good as the school system in my neighborhood.

00:59:49.750 --> 00:59:54.780
Full participation is—is you—your kids
get to go to college, just like mine get to

00:59:54.780 --> 00:59:55.780
go to college.

00:59:55.780 --> 00:59:57.560
Otherwise, you're not at the starting line.

00:59:57.560 --> 01:00:01.079
And when you can't get to the starting line,
you never get to the finish line.

01:00:01.079 --> 01:00:06.020
The genius of this country is, in fact, a
lot of ability to do for yourself and work

01:00:06.020 --> 01:00:07.470
hard to succeed.

01:00:07.470 --> 01:00:11.079
But if you can't get everybody to the starting
line then you can't succeed.

01:00:11.079 --> 01:00:14.880
And the reason for that is, going back to
what I said about income inequality.

01:00:14.880 --> 01:00:18.900
When enough people can't get to the starting
line then they stop believing in the vision

01:00:18.900 --> 01:00:20.180
of the country.

01:00:20.180 --> 01:00:24.380
That is why the Great Society was important,
because it was about getting people to the

01:00:24.380 --> 01:00:25.380
starting line.

01:00:25.380 --> 01:00:26.380
HIRSH: (inaudible)

01:00:26.380 --> 01:00:27.380
(CROSSTALK)

01:00:27.380 --> 01:00:28.380
NORQUIST: That was the goal.

01:00:28.380 --> 01:00:32.470
The reality was the opposite.

01:00:32.470 --> 01:00:33.470
(APPLAUSE)

01:00:33.470 --> 01:00:35.470
HIRSH: And the debate over the Great Society
lives on, clearly.

01:00:35.470 --> 01:00:36.470
Thank you very much.

01:00:36.470 --> 01:00:37.470
I want to remind everyone that this has been
on the record.

01:00:37.470 --> 01:00:38.470
Thank you, gentlemen, for a very enlightening
(inaudible).

01:00:38.470 --> 01:00:39.470
NORQUIST: You kept talking about...

01:00:39.470 --> 01:00:39.471
END

